  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Evaluating Integrals with Shifted Impulses",
    "lesson_core_move": "Evaluate integrals containing shifted unit impulses by checking whether the impulse time lies inside the integration limits and substituting the sampled value when it does.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Evaluating Integrals with Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Evaluating Integrals with Shifted Impulses",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A shifted impulse $\\delta(\\tau-t_0)$ tells you where to sample the signal inside the integral. The key cue is the impulse location $t_0$, because that is the only time value the impulse preserves.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)\\,\\delta(\\tau-t_0)\\,d\\tau = x(t_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a finite interval, first check whether the impulse time lies inside the limits $[a,b]$. If $t_0\\in[a,b]$, the integral returns the sample $x(t_0)$; if $t_0$ is outside the interval, the impulse contributes $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_a^b x(\\tau)\\,\\delta(\\tau-t_0)\\,d\\tau = \\begin{cases}x(t_0), & a\\le t_0\\le b \\\\ 0, & t_0<a \\text{ or } t_0>b\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the procedure is simple: read the impulse center first, test it against the integration limits, and then substitute that time into $x(\\tau)$ only when the impulse sits inside the interval. If more than one shifted impulse appears later, apply the same rule term by term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Evaluating Integrals with Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating an Integral When the Impulse Time Is Inside the Limits",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{-2}^{5} 3\\tau\\,\\delta(\\tau-1)\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse at $\\tau=1$, and $1$ lies inside the integration limits $[-2,5]$. That means the inside-case rule applies: sample the signal at the impulse time instead of integrating the signal normally.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-2}^{5} x(\\tau)\\,\\delta(\\tau-t_0)\\,d\\tau = x(t_0)\\quad\\text{when }a\\le t_0\\le b",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the signal factor is $x(\\tau)=3\\tau$, so the rule gives $x(1)$. The impulse does not change the signal shape; it only selects the value at the impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(1)=3\\cdot 1=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the integral evaluates to $3$. The same one-line substitution is the whole move when the impulse time is inside the limits.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Evaluating Integrals with Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating an Integral When the Impulse Time Is Outside the Limits",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{-2}^{1} \\cos(\\tau)\\,\\delta(\\tau-3)d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse at $\\tau=3$, with $x(\\tau)=\\cos(\\tau)$. The interval test is the only decision here: $3$ is not in $[-2,1]$, so the impulse sits outside the integration window.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3\\notin[-2,1]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When the impulse time is outside the limits, there is no sampled value to return, so the integral is zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-2}^{1} \\cos(\\tau)\\,\\delta(\\tau-3)d\\tau = 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the entire outside-case branch: check the limits first, then return $0$ if the impulse location is not included.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Evaluating Integrals with Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Evaluating Integrals with Multiple Shifted Impulses",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{-2}^{3} (\\tau+2)\\,\\delta(\\tau-1)d\\tau + 2\\int_{-2}^{3} \\delta(\\tau+4)d\\tau - \\int_{-2}^{3} 5\\,\\delta(\\tau-\\tau_0)d\\tau$ with $\\tau_0=4$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is linearity: split the expression into separate shifted-impulse terms and test each impulse time against the same limits $[-2,3]$. The impulse times are $1$, $-4$, and $4$, so only the first term lies inside the interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "1\\in[-2,3],\\quad -4\\notin[-2,3],\\quad 4\\notin[-2,3]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The inside term samples its paired signal factor at $\\tau=1$, so $\\int_{-2}^{3} (\\tau+2)\\,\\delta(\\tau-1)d\\tau = 1+2 = 3$. The other two terms contribute $0$ because their impulses are outside the interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-2}^{3} (\\tau+2)\\,\\delta(\\tau-1)d\\tau = 3,\\quad 2\\int_{-2}^{3} \\delta(\\tau+4)d\\tau = 0,\\quad \\int_{-2}^{3} 5\\,\\delta(\\tau-4)d\\tau = 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the whole expression evaluates to $3$. The rule is still the same one impulse at a time: check the bounds, sample the inside terms, and drop the outside terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
